  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "How does a sat-nav know where I am?",
      "a": "It receives signals from several GPS satellites, each saying when it was sent. From the time each takes to arrive, it works out its distance to each satellite and so its position.",
      "asks": [
        "How does GPS find my location?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why doesn't the sat-nav pick the shortest route?",
      "a": "The shortest route by distance can be slow on narrow, busy roads. Sat-navs usually add up travel times instead, so a longer route on fast roads can win.",
      "asks": [
        "Why does my sat-nav take a longer way?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How does a sat-nav choose between routes?",
      "a": "It treats roads as links with travel times and searches outward from the start, always extending the quickest route found so far, until it reaches the destination. This is the idea behind Dijkstra's algorithm.",
      "asks": [
        "What is Dijkstra's algorithm?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Does a sat-nav send signals to satellites?",
      "a": "No. GPS satellites only broadcast; your sat-nav just listens. Live traffic and map updates come separately, over the mobile network.",
      "asks": [
        "Can satellites track my sat-nav?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How does a sat-nav know about traffic jams?",
      "a": "Many sat-navs and phone apps share anonymous speed data from other users, plus road sensors and reports. Slow speeds on a road raise its travel time, and the route is recalculated.",
      "asks": [
        "How does live traffic work?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "Edsger Dijkstra devised his shortest-path algorithm in 1956.",
    "A GPS receiver needs signals from at least four satellites to fix its position and the time.",
    "GPS satellites orbit about 20,000 kilometres above the Earth."
